-- title:
--   Reduction commutes with the divisorial Hecke correspondence
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and a prime $q$ with $Nq \neq 0$, and write $k =$ the residue field of $A$. Assume the two degeneracy maps at level $N \to Nq$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ — the inclusion `heckeAlphaBar` of `modularFunctionFieldBar N` into `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` and the map `heckeBetaBar` induced by the $q$-fold expansion of Laurent exponents — are integral, and likewise their characteristic-$\ell$ counterparts `heckeAlphaC`, `heckeBetaC` from `modularFunctionFieldC k N` into `charLDegeneracyRoof k N q`, the subfield of $k((Q))$ generated by the four expansions $j(q), j(q^N), j(q^q), j(q^{Nq})$. Assume each of the three fields has principal divisors (every nonzero element has a degree-zero divisor recording its orders at all places), and that every place of the roof over $k$ has residue degree $1$. Let $R_1$ be a regular prolongation of $A$ to `modularFunctionFieldBar N` with residue field `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, and $R$ one to `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` with residue field the roof: valuation subrings contracting to $A$, with surjective residue homomorphisms whose kernels are the maximal ideals, compatible with $A$, and in which every nonzero element becomes a unit-residue integer after scaling by a constant. Let $r_1$, $r$ be maps from the generic places at level $N$, resp. $Nq$, to the places of the special-fibre level-$N$ field, resp. the roof, each carrying the divisor of any integral element with nonzero residue to the divisor of its residue, in the sense that `Finsupp.mapDomain` along the map sends a divisor computing $\operatorname{ord}_P(f)$ to one computing $\operatorname{ord}_Q(\text{residue } f)$. Assume $R$-residues are compatible with both legs, i.e. for $f \in R_1$ the elements `heckeAlphaBar f` and `heckeBetaBar f` lie in $R$ and their residues are `heckeAlphaC`, resp. `heckeBetaC`, of the residue of $f$; and that for every generic level-$N$ place $v$ the pullback of the divisor $1 \cdot v$ along `heckeAlphaBar` has the same degree as the pullback of $1 \cdot r_1(v)$ along `heckeAlphaC`, and similarly for the $\beta$-legs. Then for every place $v$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, pushing forward along $r_1$ the divisor `heckeDivBar hαq hβq (single v 1)` — the $\beta$-pullback followed by $\alpha$-pushforward of $1\cdot v$ — yields `heckeDivFibre k N q hβc hαc (single (r₁ v) 1)`, the corresponding $\beta_C$-pullback and $\alpha_C$-pushforward of $1 \cdot r_1(v)$.
--
--   This is the statement that reduction of places modulo the place determined by $A$ commutes with the divisorial Hecke correspondence $T_q = \alpha_* \beta^*$ at level $N$, derived purely from the given prolongation and place-reduction data rather than from a model over a ring. It feeds the place-specialisation arguments that compute the matrix of the Hecke correspondence on the special fibre, in the line of Shimura's reduction of modular correspondences and Igusa's good reduction of the Kroneckerian model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_mapDomain_heckeDivBar_single_eq_heckeDivFibre_of_regularProlongation.lean

